WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.650
A thousand years ago in Basra, a group of thinkers wrote a strange story: the animals take human beings to court and ask,

2
00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:16.740
"why do you imagine that we are your property?" The human defence is the very one we still use today — reason belongs to us, and therefore the world is ours.

3
00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:22.950
But is that defence really sound, or do we win only because we are both the judge and the accused in this case?

4
00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:38.800
In the middle of the tenth century, in Basra, there worked a community of thinkers who had made a principle of concealing their names.

5
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.250
They called themselves the Ikhwan al-Safa, the Brethren of Purity.

6
00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:54.580
They left behind an encyclopaedia made up of fifty-two epistles: numbers, musical intervals, the movement of the stars, the rules of logic, the descent of the soul into the body and its rise again.

7
00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:59.510
Among those fifty-two texts, one resembles none of the others.

8
00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:02.680
It is neither a textbook nor a treatise.

9
00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.370
It is a court record.

10
00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:12.220
Its subject is the case the animals bring against humankind, and the place where the case is heard is the court of the king of the jinn.

11
00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:15.230
The scene is set like this.

12
00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:20.480
In the middle of the Green Sea, somewhere near the equator, lies the island of Sa'un.

13
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:29.730
Its ruler is Biwarasp the Wise, king of the jinn; the text presents him as a sovereign who is just, generous, a refuge for the stranger.

14
00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:34.660
A ship breaks apart in a storm and human beings are cast up on this island.

15
00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:39.590
The island is fertile; they stay, they settle, they sow and reap.

16
00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:43.240
And then they lay hands on the island's older inhabitants.

17
00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:48.970
Ropes go around necks, loads onto backs, pens are built, gates are closed.

18
00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:58.700
The animals, who had roamed freely on those plains since the time of their ancestors, one day speak among themselves and resolve to choose a delegation and come before the king.

19
00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:07.310
Something happens that is rarely found in the history of literature: the animals take the plaintiff's seat, and the human being takes the chair of the accused.

20
00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:10.160
The mule speaks first.

21
00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:21.650
It describes the iron forced into its mouth, the loads piled on its back beyond what it can carry, the whip that comes when it slows; and being fed not when its belly is empty but when its work is done.

22
00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:27.060
The ox and the donkey describe a working day that begins before sunrise and ends in the dark.

23
00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:32.630
The ram speaks less of its wool being taken every year than of the thing that waits for it at the end.

24
00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:38.200
The camel speaks of thirst, the elephant of chains, the hare of being chased for sport.

25
00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:45.850
Then the bee takes the floor and lays out the finest point in the whole case: it made its honey for its own young, not to be given to anyone.

26
00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:51.900
When a human being comes and takes it, he is not accepting a gift; he is emptying out a property.

27
00:02:51.900 --> 00:03:02.430
The parrot is left for last, and it imitates the sentences human beings use to explain their own superiority; heard from its beak, those sentences ring a little strangely.

28
00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:07.040
The human side makes its defence in an orderly way, not a scattered one.

29
00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:14.210
Learned men of every nation rise in turn and build the same skeleton of argument: we are the masters, you are the servants.

30
00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:23.140
As proof they point to their upright posture, the gripping power of their fingers, speech, reasoning, the arts they practise, the cities they have built.

31
00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:27.590
They recall the trust that the sacred texts placed in humankind.

32
00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:38.440
Then they come to the proof they rely on most: your meat, your milk, your wool, your strength serve us; and if the existence of one thing serves another, then it was created for that other's sake.

33
00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:42.570
The animals' answer cuts that last proof straight through the middle.

34
00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:44.940
Benefit is no proof of ownership.

35
00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:50.670
The lion, too, eats human beings; does that mean the human being was created for the lion?

36
00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:56.080
The mosquito feeds on human blood; does the human being become the mosquito's property?

37
00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:03.570
They object to the matter of upright posture as well, but not with pride — with an inventory: our bodies are born complete.

38
00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:13.140
Our fur is on our backs, our weapons are on our faces, we find our way with no one to show it to us, and we run within a short time of coming into the world.

39
00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:21.270
The human infant, meanwhile, is born the most helpless of all living creatures; for years it must be carried, clothed, fed.

40
00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:26.840
Your arts, then, are the product not of your superiority but of your deficiency.

41
00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:33.690
You invented weaving because you had no fur, the house because you had no shelter, the weapon because you had no teeth.

42
00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:43.260
And with that same ingenuity you also invented the bit, the whip, the cage and the butcher's hook; these did not exist in nature, they arrived with you.

43
00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:49.630
The most striking thing about this trial is perhaps not what the two sides say, but who sits on the bench.

44
00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:52.800
The judge is neither human nor animal.

45
00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:58.370
He is a jinni; a third intelligence, outside both parties, bound to the interest of neither.

46
00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:02.820
The writers in Basra can hardly have chosen this by accident.

47
00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:09.510
The hearing lasts for days, the court disperses, and Biwarasp does not announce his verdict that day.

48
00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:17.320
The ground of the argument in Basra was this: are we doing the animal an injustice, and if so, how great an injustice.

49
00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:23.050
Seven centuries later, in Europe, the defence made an entirely different move.

50
00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:29.740
It stopped debating the degree of the injustice and opened up for debate the very existence of the plaintiff.

51
00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:37.230
In sixteen thirty-seven, in Holland, Descartes published his Discours de la méthode, the Discourse on the Method.

52
00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:47.280
In the fifth part of the book he describes the animal body, from beginning to end, as a mechanism: the heart is a pump, the vessels are pipes, the nerves are pull-cords,

53
00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:52.530
and the muscles are bellows that swell and shorten as fluid flows through them.

54
00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:57.460
This description did not startle the people of his day the way it startles us.

55
00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:08.310
In Descartes' youth there were water-driven grotto machines in the royal gardens of France, at Saint-Germain-en-Laye: when a visitor stepped on a certain stone, figures playing instruments would begin to move,

56
00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:12.440
a goddess would hide herself, a sea god would come forward.

57
00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:20.890
And the clock towers of the cities demonstrated daily that a gear and a spring, without knowing what they were doing, could produce an unerring order.

58
00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:23.900
From this Descartes draws two criteria.

59
00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:28.190
The first is language, though not in the sense of making sounds.

60
00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:32.320
A machine makes sounds too; touch it and it may cry out.

61
00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:39.490
What distinguishes us is that the one in front of you can arrange words afresh so as to reply appropriately to everything you say.

62
00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:45.540
The second is the difference between doing something through knowledge and doing it through the arrangement of one's organs.

63
00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:51.750
A clock keeps time better than we do, but the clock has no notion that there is such a thing as time.

64
00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:59.720
For Descartes the animal is like this: an automaton made by nature, far surpassing every device that has come from human hands.

65
00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:04.970
Here one has to be careful, because Descartes himself was more cautious than his students.

66
00:07:04.970 --> 00:07:18.060
In a letter of sixteen forty-nine to the English thinker Henry More he says it plainly: he is speaking of thought, not of sensation; and in so far as sensation depends on a bodily organ, he denies it to no animal.

67
00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:28.910
In the same letter he goes further, writing that in his view it cannot be proved that there is thought in animals, but neither can it be proved that there is none, because the human mind cannot reach inside them.

68
00:07:28.910 --> 00:07:33.680
That sentence, written in the last year of his life, is not a claim.

69
00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:36.530
It is an admission that he does not know.

70
00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:40.180
Those who came after did not carry that caution with them.

71
00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:44.630
It was a period in which the method was less attractive than the conclusion.

72
00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:55.800
One of the memoirists of the Port-Royal circle records that living animals were cut open and examined there, and that the sounds they made were received as the sound of an organ pipe or of a stretched spring.

73
00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:06.970
According to Fontenelle, the philosopher Malebranche kicked a pregnant dog that had brushed against his leg, and when the man beside him recoiled, answered: don't you know that it feels nothing at all?

74
00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:12.860
The story itself is an anecdote, but the mentality it describes is a mentality on the record.

75
00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:17.950
Put in the language of the courtroom, this is the strongest strategy the defence can find.

76
00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:23.520
Easier than proving that you are in the right is proving that there is no one across from you.

77
00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:32.290
If the scream is the noise of a spring, there is no pain; if there is no pain, there is no victim; if there is no victim, the case is dismissed.

78
00:08:32.290 --> 00:08:36.900
The sharpest answer to this defence within its own century came from Voltaire.

79
00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:48.870
In the article on animals in his Philosophical Dictionary he describes a dog that has lost its master: searching every room, pacing restlessly, and at last finding him and showing its joy with its whole body.

80
00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:58.120
Then he asks his question: to the man who nails this dog to a table and cuts it open alive — what are those nerves, the very same as your own, there for?

81
00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:00.970
To feel nothing?

82
00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:07.820
The machine defence lost its standing in that century, but it did not disappear; it only changed clothes.

83
00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:12.750
Today nobody says, in the crude form, that an animal does not feel.

84
00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:17.360
What is said is this: we cannot really know what is going on inside it.

85
00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:21.970
The strangeness of that sentence is that it works in one direction only.

86
00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:26.420
The same we cannot know is never raised about the person at the next desk.

87
00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:32.950
In seventeen eighty-nine, in England, a philosopher of law broke this closed circle with a footnote.

88
00:09:32.950 --> 00:09:41.400
In the last chapter of Jeremy Bentham's An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation, squeezed in at the bottom of a page, there are a few lines.

89
00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:45.690
Bentham begins by showing the inconsistency of the criteria.

90
00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:54.460
A full-grown horse or dog is, beyond comparison, a more rational and more communicable creature than an infant of a day or a week old.

91
00:09:54.460 --> 00:10:02.590
So if reason and speech are taken as the measure, that measure does not fall where it is wanted; it leaves the infant outside and the horse inside.

92
00:10:02.590 --> 00:10:11.040
Then he writes the famous sentence: the question is not, can they reason, nor is it, can they talk; the question is, can they suffer.

93
00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:19.490
And right beside it he sets a comparison: the blackness of the skin can be no reason for abandoning a human being to the whim of a tormentor.

94
00:10:19.490 --> 00:10:23.300
He was writing this in a world where slavery was still legal.

95
00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:27.750
What Bentham did, technically, was to shift the boundary.

96
00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:36.040
Until then the circle of morality had been drawn according to capacity: what can a being do, can it reason, can it speak, can it make a promise.

97
00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:40.970
Bentham drew the circle according to vulnerability: what can be done to that being.

98
00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:44.620
The first criterion takes in the strong.

99
00:10:44.620 --> 00:10:47.790
The second takes in the one who suffers.

100
00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:52.240
Bentham's own limits are worth stating, in the interest of honesty.

101
00:10:52.240 --> 00:11:00.850
He went on eating meat, and argued that a painless death did no wrong to the animal; his reasoning was that the animal does not anticipate its death in advance,

102
00:11:00.850 --> 00:11:04.500
and that the end awaiting it in nature is usually far worse.

103
00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:12.150
Whether that calculation is correct still sits at the very centre of the debate today — but the ledger in which the account was opened is his.

104
00:11:12.150 --> 00:11:17.880
In those same years, in Königsberg, Kant was arriving somewhere else entirely.

105
00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:25.370
In his view we have no direct duties toward animals, because duty arises only toward rational beings who are ends in themselves.

106
00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:33.820
Cruelty to an animal is wrong all the same, but only indirectly: it dulls the capacity for pity within a person and hardens them toward other people as well.

107
00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:40.510
The whipped animal, in other words, is not a victim — it is a wound opening in the character of the one wielding the whip.

108
00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:51.520
Schopenhauer could not stomach this view; he said that Kant's conception of morality treated the animal as a thing, and wrote that European philosophy had a great deal to learn from Asian thought on this question.

109
00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:55.490
In the twentieth century the footnote turned into a programme.

110
00:11:55.490 --> 00:12:06.660
In nineteen seventy, Richard Ryder gave a name to an attitude that mirrors the structure of racism: speciesism — discounting a being's interests simply because it belongs to another species.

111
00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:16.390
In nineteen seventy-five Peter Singer's Animal Liberation appeared; Singer argued not that animals should be granted human rights, but that where pain and pleasure are concerned,

112
00:12:16.390 --> 00:12:19.720
an equal interest should be given equal weight.

113
00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:29.450
In nineteen eighty-three Tom Regan went further: some animals are the subject of a life — they have memories, expectations, preferences and a future of their own;

114
00:12:29.450 --> 00:12:34.060
and a being like that cannot be a mere instrument for someone else's purposes.

115
00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:38.510
Meanwhile, the question was also being worked on in laboratories.

116
00:12:38.510 --> 00:12:48.240
Researchers measured how widespread pain receptors are among vertebrates; how fish, once harmed, do not merely flee but alter their behaviour for days afterwards;

117
00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:52.210
and how that alteration reverses when painkillers are administered.

118
00:12:52.210 --> 00:13:04.340
In twenty twelve, neuroscientists gathered at Cambridge signed a declaration on consciousness: the neural structures that generate consciousness are found not only in humans but in all mammals, in birds,

119
00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:07.830
and in certain other creatures such as the octopus.

120
00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:17.240
A similar declaration published in New York in twenty twenty-four pushed the boundary wider still, stating that the scientific grounding is strong for birds and mammals,

121
00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:26.330
and that the possibility of conscious experience must be taken seriously for reptiles, fish, cephalopods, crustaceans, and even insects.

122
00:13:26.330 --> 00:13:29.500
The upshot rests in a strange place.

123
00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:33.150
What the mule of Basra had to say can now be measured.

124
00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:38.880
The existence of suffering is documented with a certainty unimaginable two hundred years ago.

125
00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:42.210
But evidence is not standing to sue.

126
00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:46.660
A being whose suffering has been proven still has nowhere to file a petition.

127
00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:50.630
Other ways of living with that silence had been tried as well.

128
00:13:50.630 --> 00:14:04.360
In Istanbul, in Bursa, in the old cities of Anatolia, there are little stone houses carved into the walls of mosques, caravanserais, madrasas and fountains: bird palaces, complete with eaves, windows,

129
00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:07.530
sometimes even columns.

130
00:14:07.530 --> 00:14:15.500
It is architecture at the scale of a bird — something built into a wall for a bird, and not placed there as a display for anyone.

131
00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:24.270
In the records of charitable foundations one finds line items set aside for birdseed, and revenues left to buy meat for the street animals.

132
00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:32.560
In nineteen twenty-eight Ahmet Hâşim wrote about a place he saw in Bursa: the Gurabahane-i Laklakan, the house of forlorn storks.

133
00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:38.130
It was a place where storks with broken wings, unable to migrate, were cared for.

134
00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:42.100
There is a clear foundation of ideas behind this behaviour.

135
00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:48.630
The Quran says that the animals walking the earth and the birds flying on their wings are communities just as humans are.

136
00:14:48.630 --> 00:14:59.160
The body of hadith tells of a person who drew water from a well for a thirsty dog and was forgiven, and of a person who shut a cat away and let it starve and was held to account for it.

137
00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:10.970
In Ibn Tufayl's Hayy ibn Yaqzan, the man on the island reorders his own eating as he matures; he turns to fallen fruit and to seeds that have already multiplied themselves, trying, as far as he can,

138
00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:13.660
to take nothing living out of the world.

139
00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:17.310
But the registers of that same city hold another entry.

140
00:15:17.310 --> 00:15:28.480
In nineteen ten, the street dogs of Istanbul were rounded up and left on a bare rock in the Sea of Marmara — Sivriada, known in common speech as Hayırsızada, the island of no use.

141
00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:32.450
There was no water there, no shade, no food.

142
00:15:32.450 --> 00:15:39.620
Tens of thousands of animals died; by some accounts, when the wind blew from a certain direction the sound carried to the city.

143
00:15:39.620 --> 00:15:44.870
The hand that carved the bird palace and the hand that loaded that boat belong to the same culture.

144
00:15:44.870 --> 00:15:53.320
The issue is not hypocrisy but category: one was counted as a subject of compassion, the other as a line item of municipal administration.

145
00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:58.250
Which column an animal is written into determines what will be done to it.

146
00:15:58.250 --> 00:16:03.500
In the last century, a handful of animals came very close to shifting that column.

147
00:16:03.500 --> 00:16:12.750
An organisation called the Nonhuman Rights Project petitioned the courts on behalf of large animals held in zoos; what it sought was neither damages nor punishment,

148
00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:18.320
but that old legal remedy demanding that a person deprived of liberty be brought before a judge.

149
00:16:18.320 --> 00:16:28.530
In June of twenty twenty-two, the New York Court of Appeals rejected one such petition, filed for Happy, an elephant at the Bronx Zoo, by a vote of five to two.

150
00:16:28.530 --> 00:16:36.020
The majority wrote that this remedy was open to persons, and that widening its scope would bring consequences impossible to calculate.

151
00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:42.230
The judge who wrote in dissent said that the conditions in which the elephant was held amounted to an injustice.

152
00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:56.920
In Argentina, two rulings went the other way: in Buenos Aires, the description nonhuman person was applied to Sandra, an orangutan, and in twenty sixteen, in Mendoza, a petition was granted for Cecilia, a chimpanzee,

153
00:16:56.920 --> 00:16:59.930
who was sent to a sanctuary in Brazil.

154
00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:08.220
In twenty twenty, in Pakistan, the Islamabad High Court, ruling on the case of Kaavan, an elephant kept in chains for years,

155
00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:14.430
grounded its decision in Islamic sources governing the treatment of animals and ordered the elephant released.

156
00:17:14.430 --> 00:17:20.000
There is another line of development that proceeds without entering the debate over personhood at all.

157
00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:29.250
The European Union, in the thirteenth article of the Treaty of Lisbon, which came into force in two thousand nine, defined animals as sentient beings.

158
00:17:29.250 --> 00:17:36.740
In twenty twenty-two, the United Kingdom passed a sentience law covering octopuses and lobsters alongside vertebrates.

159
00:17:36.740 --> 00:17:48.870
In Turkey, the Animal Protection Law, number five thousand one hundred ninety-nine, adopted in two thousand four, was amended in twenty twenty-one to remove animals from the category of objects and property;

160
00:17:48.870 --> 00:17:53.000
acts of torture and killing were tied to prison sentences.

161
00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:58.730
While all of this was happening, the scale of the case grew to a place never seen before in history.

162
00:17:58.730 --> 00:18:09.260
Today, around eighty billion land animals are slaughtered worldwide every year; the number taken from the water is so vast that no count by head is even kept — it is measured by weight.

163
00:18:09.260 --> 00:18:13.390
And yet, in the daily life of cities, none of this makes a sound.

164
00:18:13.390 --> 00:18:22.480
The slaughterhouse moved out of the city centre to the edge, then beyond the edge of town; it went into windowless buildings, and the trucks began moving at night.

165
00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:27.410
The silence on the plate is not the absence of a scream; it is distance.

166
00:18:27.410 --> 00:18:36.660
This may be the only working form the machine defence has left today: instead of saying the animal does not feel, keeping the place where it feels outside the field of vision.

167
00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:40.950
The question of what that court in Basra decided finds its meaning here.

168
00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:44.920
Biwarasp ended the case in favour of the human beings.

169
00:18:44.920 --> 00:18:48.090
But it is worth looking at how he ended it.

170
00:18:48.090 --> 00:18:53.820
Not by refuting a single one of the things the mule, the ox, the ram and the bee had said.

171
00:18:53.820 --> 00:19:03.390
The final word from the human side was this: we are beings who will answer for what we do, a responsibility has been laid upon us, there is a test and there is a reckoning.

172
00:19:03.390 --> 00:19:06.080
The judge accepted that claim.

173
00:19:06.080 --> 00:19:12.770
Which means the reasoning behind the ruling was not that humanity is innocent, but that humanity is responsible.

174
00:19:12.770 --> 00:19:16.740
A verdict that reads like an acquittal and works like a sentence.

175
00:19:16.740 --> 00:19:23.270
Nearly everyone who reads the text notices the same thing: on every question of fact, the animals had won.

176
00:19:23.270 --> 00:19:28.040
If the same case were brought today, we are the ones who would convene the court.

177
00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:35.370
The party writing the law, appointing the judge, choosing the expert witness and making the defence is one and the same party.

178
00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:43.500
No one can be the judge of their own case; that is precisely why, a thousand years ago in Basra, a jinn was seated on the bench.

179
00:19:43.500 --> 00:19:53.550
The only honest thing we have to put in the place of that third mind is the question we hear inside our own heads standing in front of a shelf at the market, or in the moment we sit down at the table.

180
00:19:53.550 --> 00:20:01.360
The record is closed and the judgment handed down; the only line left blank is the one that says how the responsibility is to be paid.

181
00:20:01.360 --> 00:20:05.170
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

182
00:20:05.170 --> 00:20:08.660
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
